/**

@page page_exahype2_numerics_fv Finite Volume


The finite volume method (FVM) is a discretization technique for PDEs, especially those that arise from physical conservation laws.
FVM employs a volume integral formulation of the problem, utilizing a finite partitioning set of volumes to discretize the equations. Here we consider
the following 1D example:

@image html finite-volumes.png


In the 1D illustration above, we have a patch with @f$ p=5 @f$ volumes, one quantity,  and a halo of one.
That is, we are given a @f$ Q_{in} @f$ with @f$ 7 @f$ values and return @f$ 5 @f$ new values for the next time step.


If the solution per volume is constant, nothing happens within the volume per time step. If something happens with a volume, i.e., if the solution grows or goes down. Then this must be due to stuff flowing in or out of the voxel.
We can write this down as integral over each volume @f$ V @f$:

\f[
 \int _V Q_k(t+\Delta t) dx = \int _V Q_k(t) dx + \Delta t \cdot \int _V \partial _t Q_k(t) dx
\f]

The formula already applies an explicit Euler time integrator.
Next, we make the formula a little bit simpler, exploiting the fact that a volume shall have the volume length @f$ h @f$ and each volume is a square or cube, respectively.
Once again, it helps that the solution is constant per volume.

\f[
 \int _V Q_k(t+\Delta t) dx  =  \int _V Q_k(t) dx + \Delta t \cdot \int _V \partial _t Q_k(t) dx \\
 h^d Q_k(t+\Delta t)  =  h^d Q_k(t) dx + \Delta t \cdot \int _V \partial _t Q_k(t) dx \\
 h^d Q_k(t+\Delta t)  =  h^d Q_k(t) dx + \Delta t \cdot \int _V \nabla F(Q_k(t)) + S(Q_k)  dx
\f]

The last line inserts the PDE in the form

\f[
 \partial _t Q(t) = \nabla F(Q(t)) + S(Q)
\f]

and use the divergence theorem:

\f[
 h^d Q_k(t+\Delta t)  =  h^d Q_k(t) dx + \Delta t \cdot \int _V \nabla F(Q_k(t)) + S_k(Q)  dx \\
 Q_k(t+\Delta t)  =  Q_k(t) dx + \frac{\Delta t}{h^d} \cdot \left( \oint _{\partial V} (F(Q_k(t)),n) dS(x) + h^d \cdot S(Q_k) \right)
\f]

The remaining problem is that we have a discontinuity at the boundary of each voxel. To solve this problem,
we introduce the numerical flux. One cheap and stable way is to use Rusanov flux.

### Rusanov flux


\f[
    {F|}_{n}(Q) \approx \frac{1}{2} (F_n^+(Q) + F_n^-(Q)) - (Q^+ - Q^-) \cdot \text{max}(\lambda_{max,n}(Q^+),\lambda_{max,n}(Q^-))
\f]

The flux on a face @f$ {F}_{n}(Q) @f$ is the average of the flux within the left adjacent cell and the right adjacent cell.
A red circle marks this average in the sketch above.
The average alone smears shocks out quickly.
In the sketch above, the wave travels from left to right.
More flow then crosses the face from left to right than from right to left.
The difference @f$ Q^+ - Q^- @f$ corrects the average.
The "remaining" eigenvalues of a strictly hyperbolic system are all positive.
Globally, one negative eigenvalue belongs to the time dimension.
The time stepping treats it.

The correction grows with the wave speed.
A slow flow corrects the average by a tiny amount.
A fast wave corrects it by a large part of the solution gradient.
The maximum eigenvalue along the normal bounds the wave speed.
The scheme cannot evaluate this eigenvalue on the face.
It evaluates the directional eigenvalue in the left and the right volume and takes the maximum of the two.



*/
